<section class="rt-section" id="tracked">
  <h2>What is tracked</h2>
  <p>SB 63 (Stats. 2025, Ch. 740) requires MTC to conduct a two-phase financial efficiency review of four
    transit agencies: AC Transit, BART, Caltrain, and Muni (Gov. Code §67762). By July 1, 2026, each agency
    had to identify the Phase 1 strategies it commits to and adopt them as formal policy or budget actions
    (§67766(b)). If voters approve the regional measure, a second review follows, and each agency must adopt
    and follow an implementation plan as a condition of continued funding (§67766(c)-(h)).</p>
  <p>This site tracks {{ n_commitments }} Phase 1 recommendations: one entry for each “Early Action Strategy”
    action listed in an agency's section of the Phase 1 report (pp. 56-63). Phase 2 recommendations and
    implementation-plan commitments will be added when those documents are published.</p>
  <p><strong>How the count compares with MTC's.</strong> MTC reports 32 Early Action Strategies, 29 of
    them adopted in whole or in part. MTC counts by strategy heading. This site lists each action
    separately, so AC Transit's speed-and-reliability strategy appears as three entries and SFMTA's as two.
    Those three extra entries account for the difference between 32 and {{ n_commitments }}.</p>
  <p>The report's “Strategies for Future Consideration” are not tracked. The report states they “are not
    considered Early Action Strategies for the purposes of Operator adoption under Senate Bill 63” (p. 49).
    Where an agency section lists an action that the report's summary tables label as a strategy for future
    consideration, the entry is tracked and its notes say so.</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="statuses">
  <h2>Status definitions</h2>
  <p>Each entry has exactly one status. Badges always show a text label; color is only a secondary cue.</p>
  <dl class="rt-status-defs">
    {% for s in statuses %}
    <dt>{{ badge(s.key) }}</dt>
    <dd>{{ s.definition }}</dd>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="adopted-implemented">
  <h2>Adopted versus implemented</h2>
  <p><strong>Adopted</strong> means the agency's governing board voted to commit to the recommendation.
    It is a statement of intent. Most Phase 1 recommendations are studies or plans, so adoption alone
    produces no savings.</p>
  <p><strong>Implemented</strong> requires a primary source showing the recommended action is complete.
    For a recommended study, that means the study has been delivered to the board or published. For a
    recommended policy, price, or operating change, it means the change is in effect. A study that is
    complete is marked implemented even if the agency then decides not to act on its findings; the notes
    record what the agency decided.</p>
  <p><strong>In progress</strong> sits between them. It requires documented work, such as an awarded
    contract, a launched pilot, or an approved rate change that has not fully taken effect. Staff
    statements that work is “underway,” without more detail, are noted but do not change the status by
    themselves.</p>
  <p>MTC has added the adopted Phase 1 strategies to its Productivity Improvement Program (MTC Res. 4743,
    revised Aug. 26, 2026), with an estimated completion date of June 2029 for each. Agencies report
    progress in their annual Transportation Development Act and State Transit Assistance funding claims.
    Those reports are a primary source for status changes.</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="sources">
  <h2>Sources and evidence rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every entry and milestone links to at least one primary source: the statute, the review report,
      or a document published by MTC or the agency (board agendas, staff reports, resolutions, minutes,
      official news releases, and other agency documents). News media coverage is not used as a source.</li>
    <li>The review report alone cannot establish a status. Any status other than “Unverified” requires a
      board item or other agency or MTC document.</li>
    <li>Statuses are not inferred. If sources conflict or are unclear, the entry stays “Unverified” and its
      notes describe the conflict.</li>
    <li>Dollar figures appear only where a source states them. The Phase 1 report does not estimate
      savings for individual strategies, so Phase 1 entries show “Not estimated.” Figures the report gives
      for past savings are not reused as estimates.</li>
    <li>Page numbers refer to the page of the PDF file, which can differ from the number printed on the page.</li>
    <li>A copy of the chaptered statute is kept at
      <a href="{{ base }}{{ cfg.statute_local_copy }}">{{ cfg.statute_local_copy }}</a>, because the
      Legislature's website sometimes blocks automated access.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="checking">
  <h2>How often the data is checked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each entry shows a “last verified” date: the last date someone re-read its sources and confirmed
      its status.</li>
    <li>Entries are re-checked at least monthly, and after any MTC, oversight committee, or agency board
      meeting with an SB 63 item on its agenda.</li>
    <li>An automated check runs weekly. It tests every source link and opens a public issue in the
      <a href="{{ cfg.repo_url }}/issues">project repository</a> when a link breaks.</li>
    <li>Every change to the data is recorded in the <a href="{{ base }}changelog/">changelog</a>, which is
      generated from the version history of the data files.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="scope">
  <h2>Agencies outside the review</h2>
  <p>The efficiency review covers 4 of the 15 agencies that receive a direct share of measure revenue
    under Gov. Code §67750(b)(2)-(16). The other 11 are outside the review and outside this tracker's
    scope; SB 63's efficiency-review requirements do not apply to them. (MTC's fare, accessibility, and
    wayfinding programs, §67750(b)(10)-(12), are also not tracked.)</p>
  <ul>
    {% for o in out_of_scope %}
    <li>{{ o.name }} <span class="rt-cite">({{ o.basis }}{% if o.moe %}; maintenance of effort applies, §67770{% endif %})</span></li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p class="rt-meta">Source: <a href="{{ cfg.statute_url }}">SB 63</a>, Gov. Code §67700(b), (h) (PDF p. 4)
    and §67750(b) (PDF pp. 9-12).</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="corrections">
  <h2>Corrections policy</h2>
  <p>If you believe an entry is wrong or out of date, email
    <a href="mailto:{{ cfg.contact_email }}?subject=Correction%3A%20RTM%20tracker">{{ cfg.contact_email }}</a>
    or <a href="{{ cfg.repo_url }}/issues/new">open an issue</a>. Please include the entry ID, shown at the
    bottom of each expanded entry, and a link to the primary source.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Requests are reviewed within five business days.</li>
    <li>Agencies and MTC are held to the same standard as everyone else: a correction needs a primary source.</li>
    <li>When a correction changes a status, the entry's notes say what was corrected and when. Earlier
      versions are never deleted; the changelog shows the old and new values.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="about">
  <h2>Who maintains this site</h2>
  <p>This tracker is made by <a href="{{ cfg.maintainer_url }}">{{ cfg.maintainer }}</a>, who lives in San Mateo and
    writes about transportation and housing at maxmautner.com. He supports public transit, and his essays on that
    site take positions. This tracker does not: it is not affiliated with or funded by MTC, any transit agency, or
    any campaign, and it takes no position on the measure.</p>
  <p>To keep it reliable, every status is held to the same evidence rule whether it reflects well or poorly on an
    agency: a primary source, linked, with a page number. The data and every edit to it are
    <a href="{{ cfg.repo_url }}">public</a>, so anyone can check the work, and corrections follow the
    <a href="#corrections">policy above</a>.</p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
